  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon\n\nScope note up front: `web/` is fully implemented and generally **well-optimized already** (batched queries, `Promise.all`, proper cleanup). `engine/pipeline/*` and `engine/db.py` are still stubs (`raise NotImplementedError` — Phase 2/3 per AGENTS.md), so for the engine I'm flagging forward-looking risks tied to what's already documented in AGENTS.md rather than bugs in code that doesn't exist yet.\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns\n\n**None found in `web/`.** `lib/server/conflicts.ts` and `lib/server/export.ts` already do the right thing — joins instead of per-row lookups, and `countChildrenByParent` (`entities.ts:28`) is one grouped query for a whole page rather than one per row. Worth calling out as a pattern to keep, not break, as new screens get added.\n\n**Missing indexes will bite before N+1 does**, though. `schema.ts` only has an explicit index on `entities` (`entities_run_source_external_idx`). These hot paths have none:\n\n| Table.column | Used by | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| `conflicts.runId` | every queue/export query, filtered + joined | full scan per run as conflicts grow |\n| `matches.runId`, `matches.entityAId/B` | `baseQuery()` joins in `conflicts.ts:109` | join fan-out with no index to seek on |\n| `resolutions.conflictId` | left-joined on every queue row | scan instead of seek |\n| `auditLog.runId` | `getAuditPage`, `listRunAudit` | audit screen scans grow unbounded (append-only table) |\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/db/schema.ts — add alongside the existing entities index\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", { ... }, (table) => [\n  index(\"conflicts_run_severity_idx\").on(table.runId, table.severity),\n]);\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", { ... }, (table) => [\n  uniqueIndex(\"resolutions_conflict_id_idx\").on(table.conflictId), // also closes the\n  // duplicate-join gap the comment in conflicts.ts:62-66 already calls out\n]);\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", { ... }, (table) => [\n  index(\"audit_log_run_created_idx\").on(table.runId, table.createdAt),\n]);\n```\n\nThe `resolutions.conflictId` **unique** index isn't just perf — the code comment at `conflicts.ts:62-66` already flags that without it, two resolutions per conflict would double-count and duplicate rows in the queue.\n\n**Engine, forward-looking:** `db.py`'s `write_entities`/`write_matches`/`write_conflicts` take `list[...]` — good, that's the right shape for a bulk write. When implementing, use `psycopg.extras`/`executemany` with a single `COPY` or multi-row `INSERT ... VALUES (...), (...)` rather than looping row-by-row — at 500 accounts × 2 sides, a per-row insert loop across `entities`+`matches`+`conflicts` is exactly the N+1-on-write pattern AGENTS.md's blocking-must-be-O(n·k) warning is trying to avoid on the read side.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unnecessary re-renders in React\n\n**Real issue — queue rows aren't memoized, and the callback that's passed to them is rebuilt every render**, which is the thing that would normally let memoization help:\n\n```tsx\n// web/components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx:58-63\nconst submitFor = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string): ReviewSubmit =>\n    (action: ReviewAction) => submit(conflictId, action),   // ← new closure every call\n  [submit],\n);\n// ...later, per row:\nsubmit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}   // new function identity every render, every row\n```\n\nWith up to 400 rows in the queue (`ConflictDenseTable`, `ConflictQueue` ledger view), pressing `j`/`k` to move selection changes one piece of parent state and currently re-renders the entire row list, because nothing downstream is memoized and every row gets a brand-new `submit` prop regardless. Fix:\n\n```tsx\n// Pass conflictId + a stable submit down instead of pre-binding it\n<ConflictDiffRow key={row.conflictId} row={row} onSubmit={submit} ... />\n\n// Inside ConflictDiffRow / dense table row:\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ row, onSubmit, ... }) {\n  const handleSubmit = useCallback(\n    (action: ReviewAction) => onSubmit(row.conflictId, action),\n    [onSubmit, row.conflictId],\n  );\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nThat turns a 400-row re-render into a 1-row re-render on selection/keyboard navigation — directly serves the \"reviewer working a queue of 400 should never touch the mouse\" requirement in AGENTS.md, since keyboard nav is the hot path.\n\n**Minor:** `staggerIndex` in `conflict-queue.tsx:137` is wrapped in `useMemo` with no dependency array reason — it's a pure closure over nothing, `useMemo(() => fn, [])` buys nothing here since `fn` is cheap and stable either way. Harmless, just a needless indirection.\n\n**Positive pattern to keep:** the `Elapsed` clock in `run-live.tsx:98-118` correctly isolates its `setInterval` re-render to a leaf component instead of ticking the whole `RunProgress` tree — that's the right call and worth reusing as the template for any future live-updating UI.\n\n**Minor:** `RunProgress` (`run-live.tsx:46-60`) polls every 2s with `staleTime: 0`; TanStack Query returns a new object reference each fetch even when the underlying stats are unchanged (e.g., between \"matching\" stage ticks), so `StatGrid`/`RunStageBar` re-render every 2s regardless of whether anything changed. Low-impact (small tree, low frequency) but if `StatGrid` grows heavier, add a `select` to only re-render on actual field changes.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n- **Biggest lever, already named in AGENTS.md but not yet built:** `engine/agent/graph.py`'s `resolve()` is the one that will call Claude per-conflict. AGENTS.md § Model Selection already mandates prompt caching on the ruleset block since it's identical across every call in a run — make sure that's wired via the Anthropic SDK's cache-control breakpoints on the survivorship-rule prompt segment when `propose` is implemented. This is the single largest cost/latency lever documented for this project; don't let it slip when Phase 3 lands.\n- **Read-heavy, rarely-changing data hits Postgres on every request with no caching layer:** `listSources()` and `listRulesets()` are called from multiple pages (`new-run-form`, `sources/page.tsx`, `rules/page.tsx`) and `connection()` intentionally opts out of static rendering (per the comment in `sources.ts:15-17`, this is correct — sources are request-time state). But that means *zero* caching, not even React's per-request dedup, if the same function is called twice in one render tree. Worth wrapping in `React.cache()`:\n  ```ts\n  import { cache } from \"react\";\n  export const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => { ... });\n  ```\n  This dedupes calls *within* a single request (e.g., layout + page both need sources) without violating the \"always fresh\" requirement — it doesn't cache across requests.\n- **Query-count reduction in `getQueuePage`** (`conflicts.ts:172-200`): 3 parallel queries (`selectQueueRows`, `getQueueCounts` — itself 2 queries — , `countFiltered`) all filter the same `conflicts`/`resolutions` join on `runId`. `Promise.all` already keeps latency bounded by the slowest query, so this isn't urgent, but at high request volume it's 4 round trips where a single query with `COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE ...)` window aggregates could return rows + counts + total in one pass. Worth doing if the queue becomes a high-QPS endpoint; not worth doing today given the code's existing care about correctness (chip counts each omit their own facet — collapsing this into one query is more error-prone for a marginal win).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory leaks\n\n**None found in the current `web/` code** — every subscription is cleaned up correctly:\n- `conflict-queue.tsx:130-131` — `window.addEventListener(\"keydown\", ...)` has a matching `removeEventListener` in the effect cleanup.\n- `run-live.tsx:106-107` — `setInterval` is cleared on unmount/dep change.\n\n**Forward-looking risk for the engine**, based on the stack named in AGENTS.md:\n- `sentence-transformers` model instances are large (100MB+ resident). If `blocking.py`'s `block()`/`score()` load a fresh `SentenceTransformer(...)` per call instead of once at process startup, that's an unbounded memory climb across the life of the FastAPI process, not a \"leak\" in the GC sense but the same practical effect. Load it once in `main.py` at app startup and pass it down, don't instantiate per-run.\n- `db.py` currently has no connection pool. If each `write_*` function opens its own `psycopg` connection, under concurrent runs (or retries) connections can pile up unclosed on error paths. Use `psycopg_pool.ConnectionPool` initialized once at app startup, not raw `psycopg.connect()` per call.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\n- `DiffValue` (`diff-value.tsx`) calls `diffSegments()` on every render with no memoization — two calls per visible row (side A vs B). At O(n) over short field values this is genuinely cheap (a few dozen chars), so not worth memoizing on its own, but it compounds with the re-render issue in §2: fixing the row-memoization problem also fixes this for free, since a row that doesn't re-render doesn't recompute its diff either. Don't memoize this in isolation — fix the parent instead.\n- Nothing else stood out as duplicated work — `applyDecisions` in `export.ts:380-399` builds one `Map` and does a single pass over accounts, `getExportSizing` vs `buildRunExport` compute different things (counts vs. full rows) so that's not redundancy, it's two different questions.\n\n---\n\n### Priority order if you want me to implement\n\n1. Add the three missing indexes (`conflicts.runId+severity`, unique `resolutions.conflictId`, `auditLog.runId+createdAt`) — cheap migration, closes a correctness gap too.\n2. Memoize queue rows + stabilize the `submit` callback — real re-render cost at the 400-row scale the design doc targets.\n3. Everything else is either already fine or applies to code that doesn't exist yet (engine) — I'd hold those until Phase 2/3 actually lands rather than pre-optimizing stubs.\n\nWant me to implement #1 and #2?\n",
